---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---
<DocsLayout title="Repo Management — Band Docs">
  <h1>Repo Management</h1>
  <p>
    Repos in Band represent git repositories that you want to manage with coding agent
    worktrees. Every repo must be a valid git repository. Band does not create or modify
    your repositories &mdash; it simply registers them and manages worktrees within them.
  </p>

  <figure class="my-8 mx-auto max-w-md overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-border shadow-lg">
    <img
      src="/screenshot-dashboard-overview.png"
      alt="The Band desktop dashboard showing two registered repos, band and sidepanel, each with their worktrees, agent status indicators, and git status badges."
      width="447"
      height="228"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
      class="block h-auto w-full"
    />
    <figcaption class="border-t border-border bg-card/50 px-4 py-2 text-center text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      Two registered repos in the dashboard, each with their worktrees and live status.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Adding Repos</h2>
  <p>
    There are two ways to register a repo with Band:
  </p>

  <h3>From the Dashboard UI</h3>
  <p>
    Click the <strong>+</strong> button in the top-right corner of the dashboard toolbar. A dialog
    will appear where you can select a directory. Band validates that the selected path is a git
    repository and detects the default branch automatically.
  </p>

  <h3>From the CLI</h3>
  <p>
    Use the <code>band repos add</code> command to register an existing git repository:
  </p>
  <pre><code>band repos add /path/to/your/repo
band repos add /path/to/your/repo --label my-label</code></pre>
  <p>
    The CLI detects the repository name and default branch. You can optionally assign a label
    at registration time using the <code>--label</code> flag.
  </p>

  <h2>Removing Repos</h2>
  <p>
    Removing a repo from Band unregisters it from the dashboard. It does <strong>not</strong>
    delete the repository or any of its files. You can remove a repo by:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Right-clicking the repo name and selecting <strong>Remove from list</strong>.</li>
    <li>Using the CLI: <code>band repos remove &lt;name&gt;</code></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Reordering Repos</h2>
  <p>
    Repos in the sidebar can be reordered by drag-and-drop. On desktop, click and drag a
    repo name up or down to rearrange. On mobile, press and hold a repo until it lifts,
    then drag it to the new position. The new order is persisted automatically.
  </p>

  <h2>Collapsing Repos</h2>
  <p>
    Click the chevron next to a repo name to collapse or expand its worktrees. Collapse
    state is remembered between reloads, so repos you rarely look at can stay tucked
    away. Label section headers can be collapsed the same way.
  </p>

  <h2>Labels and Filtering</h2>
  <p>
    Repos can be organized using color-coded labels. Labels are created in the
    <a href="/docs/settings">Settings</a> page, and then assigned to repos via the
    right-click context menu.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Right-click a repo name to see the <strong>Set label</strong> submenu.</li>
    <li>Select a label to assign it, or choose <strong>None</strong> to remove the label.</li>
    <li>Use the label filter dropdown in the toolbar to show only repos with a specific label.</li>
    <li>When labels are defined, repos are visually grouped by label with colored section headers.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    You can also drag repos between label groups to reassign labels quickly.
  </p>

  <h2>Repo Configuration</h2>
  <p>
    Each repo can have a <code>.band/config.json</code> file in its repository root. This
    file configures setup and teardown scripts that run during worktree lifecycle events.
  </p>
  <pre><code set:html={`{
  "setup": "npm install",
  "teardown": "docker compose down"
}`}></code></pre>
  <p>
    The <code>setup</code> script runs automatically after a worktree is created. The
    <code>teardown</code> script runs before a worktree is removed. Both are non-fatal &mdash;
    if they fail, the operation continues.
  </p>

  <h2>Context Menu</h2>
  <p>
    Right-clicking a repo name reveals additional actions:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Set label</strong> &mdash; Assign or remove a color-coded label.</li>
    <li><strong>Copy path</strong> &mdash; Copy the repo's filesystem path to the clipboard.</li>
    <li><strong>Open in Finder</strong> &mdash; Reveal the repo directory in Finder.</li>
    <li><strong>Remove from list</strong> &mdash; Unregister the repo from Band.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
